      "attributes": {
        "class": "view-mode-section view-mode-section__branch view-mode-section__branch--human"
      },
      "html": "<div class=\"w-full bg-chalk\"><section class=\"blog-reading-shell pointer-events-auto\" data-v-1c6297c3><nav class=\"blog-breadcrumb\" aria-label=\"Breadcrumb\"><a href=\"/feed\">Blogs</a><span aria-hidden=\"true\">/</span><span>Content strategy</span></nav><article aria-labelledby=\"blog-39-title\"><header class=\"blog-article-header\"><p class=\"blog-eyebrow\">Paid Media &amp; Content</p><h1 id=\"blog-39-title\">Improve existing content before publishing more</h1><p class=\"blog-article-summary\">Review useful existing pages for gaps, duplication and outdated information before commissioning another round of articles.</p><p class=\"blog-byline\">Fyntix Editorial · Updated <time datetime=\"2026-11-24\">24 Nov 2026</time> · 2 min read</p></header><nav class=\"blog-contents\" aria-label=\"On this page\"><p>On this page</p><ol><li><a href=\"#blog-39-section-1\">Revisit the reader's decision</a></li><li><a href=\"#blog-39-section-2\">Decide whether to update or consolidate</a></li><li><a href=\"#blog-39-section-3\">Add useful expertise and evidence</a></li><li><a href=\"#blog-39-section-4\">Review the role of the page afterwards</a></li></ol></nav><div class=\"blog-prose\"><p>An existing article can be close to useful while still leaving the reader's main question unresolved. Publishing another page on the same topic may create more material without fixing that gap. Review the content you already have before deciding what to produce next.</p>\n<p>Choose pages connected to important services or recurring customer questions. The aim is to improve the usefulness of the library, rather than refresh every page because it has reached a particular age.</p>\n<h2 id=\"blog-39-section-1\">Revisit the reader's decision</h2>\n<p>Write down who the page helps and what they should understand after reading it. Compare that purpose with the actual content. Identify information that is missing, difficult to find or no longer accurate.</p>\n<p>For an illustrative buying guide, the business may explain the benefits of a service without addressing suitability or preparation. Those omissions can be more important than the number of sections or the length of the introduction.</p>\n<h2 id=\"blog-39-section-2\">Decide whether to update or consolidate</h2>\n<p>Review other pages covering the same question. Some may serve distinct audiences and deserve a clearer difference. Others may repeat an answer without adding useful context. Make the decision around the reader's needs and the site's structure.</p>\n<p>Where pages are combined or removed, plan the URL and internal-link handling with the website team. Content decisions should reach the live site coherently rather than leave readers following outdated paths.</p>\n<h2 id=\"blog-39-section-3\">Add useful expertise and evidence</h2>\n<p>Ask the relevant specialist for examples, explanations and common misunderstandings. Replace broad claims with information a reader can assess. Check the context of any results and update references when the underlying information has changed.</p>\n<p>Avoid changing the date as a substitute for improving the page. A revision should make the answer more accurate, complete or useful. Keep a record of substantive changes so later reviews have context.</p>\n<h2 id=\"blog-39-section-4\">Review the role of the page afterwards</h2>\n<p>Look at the relevant search questions, visits and routes readers take through the site. Ask whether the updated content helps sales or service staff explain recurring issues. The review should reflect the purpose agreed for the page.</p>\n<p>Fyntix's content strategy connects articles and blogs with service information, search discovery and the customer journey. Improving a useful existing page can be a focused next step when the business already has material that deserves a clearer answer, rather than another version of the same introduction.</p></div><aside class=\"blog-service-callout\" aria-label=\"Work with Fyntix\"><p class=\"blog-eyebrow\">Put the idea into practice</p><h2>Plan your next step with Fyntix.</h2><p>Connect your audience and offer with a practical plan for search visibility, paid media and useful content.</p><div class=\"blog-callout-links\"><a href=\"/contact\" class=\"inline-flex items-center justify-center rounded-full\">Book a free strategy call</a><a href=\"/services#service-content-strategy\">Explore the relevant service</a></div></aside></article></section></div>"
